[ 4.1 The Phantom Protocol: SLAAC and Unmonitored Shadow Backdoors ]

Let's be honest: IPv6 is like that weird, distant uncle who shows up uninvited
to Thanksgiving dinner expecting roast. Most sysadmins ignore its existence, but
modern OSes have it enabled by default. Enter SLAAC (Stateless Address
Autoconfiguration).

Instead of obediently waiting for a traditional DHCP lease, SLAAC allows devices
to auto-configure. They spawn shadow addresses on their own, which aren't logged
anywhere in your L2/L3 topology. If your firewall doesn't explicitly log and
drop these packets, the malware gains an invisible, unmonitored backdoor. It
sends ICMPv6 Router Solicitations and suddenly, your "isolated" endpoint sprouts
wings and talks to the outside world. It is an absolute visibility nightmare.

[ 4.2 VPN Leaks & Dual-Stack Nightmares ]

As we broke down in Part 2, a default-deny policy on outbound traffic is
critical. You've built your ultimate L4 choke point and you are
aggressively dropping Port 53. But what happens when the machine has
dual-stack interfaces?

Here is the brutal truth: those iptables rules you painstakingly wrote to stop
data exfiltration apply ONLY to IPv4. If you haven't configured ip6tables with
the exact same level of paranoia (and let's not kid ourselves, nobody does), the
attacker is laughing at you.

Advanced malware understands dual-stack. It binds to a raw IPv6 socket and
routes its C2 queries right through the VPN tunnel like a VIP, completely
bypassing your supposed firewall. The VPN simply degrades into an overpriced
pipe, acting as an encrypted conduit for your own data exfiltration.

[ 4.3 The Kernel Assassination: Purging IPv6 via sysctl ]

The solution here isn't "polite" filtering. The solution is the absolute
lobotomy of the protocol. In a Zero-Trust architecture, we do not
leave doors half-open. We must purge IPv6 directly at the kernel level.
We don't just cut off its access; we revoke its right to exist.

We open /etc/sysctl.conf (or better yet, drop a custom file in
/etc/sysctl.d/99-kill-ipv6.conf) and hammer in the following:

net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6 = 1
net.ipv6.conf.default.disable_ipv6 = 1
net.ipv6.conf.lo.disable_ipv6 = 1

With a quick $ sysctl -p, the kernel stops recognizing what IPv6 even means.
The L3 stack is completely demolished. No bind sockets, no routes, no leaks.

[ 4.4 The Daemon Double-Tap: Enforcing Policy Persistence ]

If you think you are done just by throwing sysctl commands around, you are dead
wrong. NetworkManager is like a B-movie zombie: it loves restoring default
states when you aren't looking. If your interface bounces (e.g., a Wi-Fi
reconnect), the daemon might try to resurrect the stack, causing race
conditions, state restoration, or kernel panics.

The fundamental rule of malware survival is the "Double-Tap." We must enforce
policy persistence directly within the NetworkManager connection profiles.

$ nmcli connection modify "Wired connection 1" ipv6.method disabled
$ nmcli connection up "Wired connection 1"

This strips the IPv6 logic out of the L2/L3 orchestration itself. Now, neither
the kernel nor the daemon has the slightest clue how to route AAAA records.
Your endpoint is now 100% IPv4, absolutely controlled, and ready to dump all its
garbage traffic into our upstream sinkholes.

